# Case Study Plan: Partisanship & Economic Perception

## Executive Summary

How much of what Americans say about "the economy" is about the economy, and how much is about who holds the White House?

The plan is to use independents and personal-finance questions as two built-in "reality checks" to separate the partisan component of economic perception from the fundamentals, then test whether either one shows up in what households do.

## Problem Framing and Research Questions

| RQ# | Question | Hypothesis |
| --- | --- | --- |
| RQ1 | How much do economic ratings shift when the president's party changes? | Ratings swing sharply toward the incoming president's party within weeks, with no matching change in fundamentals |
| RQ2 | After removing partisanship, do perceptions still track fundamentals? | Independents' ratings (and the common year effect) move with unemployment, inflation and real earnings |
| RQ3 | Is partisanship weaker for people's own finances than for "the economy"? | The partisan gap on personal finances is smaller than on the national economy |
| RQ4 | Do partisan swings in sentiment show up in household behavior? | Aggregate saving and borrowing move with fundamentals, not with partisan sentiment |

